# Loose Radiant Cut Diamonds: Lab 2ct Costs Less Than Natural 1ct

Buying a loose radiant cut diamond means you pick the stone before you pick the setting. That is the right order. A stone you understand — proportions, certification, grade — will always look better in a setting than a stone you bought because the ring already looked nice in the display case.

The challenge with loose radiant cut diamonds is that there is no overall cut grade to rely on. GIA does not issue cut grades for fancy shapes. That means every stone requires the buyer to read table %, depth %, and the L:W ratio from the certificate before making a decision.

The market has plenty of stones that look well-priced on paper but underperform in light return because depth is too high or table is outside the ideal zone.

This guide covers natural and lab-grown loose radiant cut diamonds with real certified inventory prices — from a GIA-certified 1ct natural G-VS2 at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) to a lab-grown 2ct E-VVS1 IGI at [$2,220](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). By the end, you will know which tier to buy in, what certification to require, how radiant compares to other loose stone shapes, and what proportion numbers to verify before confirming any purchase.

![Loose radiant cut diamond complete buyer's guide](/images/loose-radiant-cut-diamond-featured.avif)

## Quick Answer

**Natural loose radiant cut diamonds** start at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a GIA-certified 1ct G-VS2. The best-value zone is G-VS2 to F-VS1 at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)–[$2,787](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29538475?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for 1ct — eye-clean, near-colorless, GIA-certified.

**Lab-grown loose radiant cut diamonds** start at [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a 1ct E-VVS1 IGI. A lab 2ct E-VVS1 IGI costs [$2,220](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — about what a 1ct natural G-VS2 costs.

**The most important thing to check on any loose radiant cut:** depth %. A stone above 70% depth looks smaller than its carat weight. Target 61–67%. Confirm this number on the certificate before everything else.

→ Start with a [GIA 1ct G-VS2 at $2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for natural, or an [IGI 1ct E-VVS1 at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for lab — both are eye-clean, certified entry points. Confirm depth is 61–67% on the certificate before buying.

## What Is a Loose Radiant Cut Diamond?

A loose radiant cut diamond is a natural or lab-grown diamond sold as a stone only — no ring, no pendant, no setting of any kind. You purchase the stone, then place it into a setting of your choosing, either through a jeweler who performs the setting work or through a platform where you pair a verified stone with a ring style.

The radiant cut itself was designed in 1977 by Henry Grossbard. It is a hybrid shape — a rectangular or square outline with a brilliant-cut facet arrangement underneath the table. The 70-facet structure gives it the crushed-ice sparkle of a round diamond with the elongated silhouette of a step-cut shape like an emerald cut.

Radiant cut diamonds can be bought as loose natural stones, loose lab-grown stones, or loose fancy-color stones — the same proportion rules apply to all three.

For the full shape guide and ring styling context, see the [radiant cut engagement rings](/radiant-cut-engagement-rings) pillar.

## The Loose Stone Advantage

**The Loose Stone Advantage** is the core reason buyers choose to purchase a stone before choosing a setting: you control both decisions independently.

When a diamond is already set in a ring at a retailer, you cannot read the depth percentage visually. You cannot compare it side-by-side with a stone at a lower price point. You cannot request the certificate plot and check where inclusions are positioned.

When you buy loose, you filter by table %, depth %, L:W ratio, color, and clarity — and you shortlist three or four stones by certificate before choosing.

The second part of the advantage is timing. You can buy the stone when inventory and price are favorable, then set it months later when you have chosen a jeweler or style. The stone's character does not change in storage.

Its proportions, color, and clarity are fixed. Nothing about the loose stone purchase expires.

## Natural vs Lab — The Price Reality

The price gap between natural and lab-grown loose radiant cut diamonds is real and consistent at every carat level. Lab-grown radiant cuts trade at 60–80% below natural equivalents at the same grade, based on certified market inventory.

A 1ct natural G-VS2 GIA costs [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A 1ct lab E-VVS1 IGI costs [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — that is a better grade (E vs G, VVS1 vs VS2) at 63% less. At 2ct, the gap widens: a natural D-VS2 GIA costs [$17,714](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/16750163?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) while a lab 2ct E-VVS1 IGI costs [$2,220](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — over 87% less for a comparable (or higher) grade.

| Stone | Grade | Carat | Cert | Price | Per-Carat | What this means |
|-------|-------|-------|------|-------|-----------|-----------------|
| [Natural Radiant](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | G-VS2 | 1ct | GIA | $2,318 | $2,318 | Entry natural. Eye-clean, near-colorless. |
| [Lab Radiant](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VVS1 | 1ct | IGI | $867 | $867 | Better grade at 63% less than entry natural. |
| [Natural Radiant](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29651855?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | G-VS2 | 1.5ct | GIA | $7,704 | $5,136 | 1.5ct natural entry. Per-carat already 2.2× the 1ct rate. |
| [Lab Radiant](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29569681?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | F-VVS1 | 1.5ct | IGI | $1,510 | $1,007 | 1.5ct lab. Less than a 1ct natural G-VS2. |
| [Natural Radiant](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/16750163?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | D-VS2 | 2ct | GIA | $17,714 | $8,857 | 2ct natural entry. Per-carat nearly 4× the 1ct rate. |
| [Lab Radiant](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VVS1 | 2ct | IGI | $2,220 | $1,110 | 2ct lab at better grade than 2ct natural entry. 87% cheaper. |

**What this means:** The lab-grown price advantage compounds with every carat increase. At 1ct, lab is 63% less than natural at the same carat weight. At 2ct, lab is 87% less. This is not a quality discount — it is a rarity and origin premium. Lab-grown diamonds carry the same physical and optical properties as natural diamonds at equivalent proportions.

**Verdict:** Natural for rarity, resale stability, and family heirloom value. Lab for maximum grade and size per dollar, when origin is not the deciding factor. The face-up appearance of a well-proportioned lab radiant and a well-proportioned natural radiant at the same carat weight and grade is identical.

![Natural vs lab radiant cut diamond price comparison — 1ct, 1.5ct, and 2ct GIA natural vs IGI lab prices](/images/loose-radiant-cut-diamond-natural-vs-lab-price-comparison.avif)

## The GIA-Only Rule

**The GIA-Only Rule** is the single most important guideline for buying a loose natural radiant cut diamond: only accept GIA certification. Do not purchase natural radiant cut diamonds certified by EGL, IGS, GCAL (when used as the only certification), or uncertified sellers, regardless of how attractive the price appears.

GIA's grading standards are the most conservative in the industry. A GIA G-VS2 is reliably a G-VS2. An EGL G-VS2 is often a GIA H or I color — EGL is documented to grade 1–2 color levels and 1–2 clarity levels above where GIA would place the same stone.

In the resale market and among professional setters who work with loose stones daily, GIA is the standard. An EGL-certified stone is typically priced — and valued by jewelers — as if it were 1–2 grades lower than stated.

For lab-grown radiant cut diamonds, IGI is the accepted standard. GIA also certifies lab-grown diamonds, and a GIA lab certificate carries the strictest grading. But IGI has been certifying lab-grown stones since the industry's earliest growth phase, and IGI lab-grown certification is accepted and trusted across all major retailers.

An IGI lab-grown D-VVS1 is a reliable D-VVS1 — buying it for a loose stone purchase is the right call. See the [lab-grown radiant cut diamond](/lab-grown-radiant-cut-diamond) guide for more on lab certification standards.

## How to Grade a Loose Radiant Cut Diamond

Grading a loose radiant cut diamond requires evaluating four factors: color, clarity, proportions, and certification. None of these can be shortcut — GIA issues no overall cut grade for radiant cuts, so each element must be read separately.

### Color: What to Target

For a loose natural radiant cut, G is the value target. G color is near-colorless, and the crushed-ice facet pattern of the radiant cut scatters light across 70 facets in a way that masks faint warmth better than a step-cut shape. In white gold or platinum, a G radiant looks white face-up to most buyers.

H is acceptable in yellow gold, where the setting's warm tone complements the stone's slight warmth and the contrast is undetectable. F and above are visually cleaner but command a meaningful premium — a 1ct F-VS2 natural at [$2,597](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28954628?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs $279 more than the G-VS2 at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), for a difference that most buyers cannot identify in a ring.

For lab-grown, target D or E. Lab-grown D and E color stones are abundant and relatively affordable — a 1ct D-VVS1 IGI is [$932](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24946874?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), only $65 more than the E-VVS1 at [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). There is no reason to settle for G or H in lab-grown when D-E costs nearly the same.

### Clarity: What to Target

VS2 is the minimum for a natural loose radiant cut diamond purchased without seeing the stone in person. VS2 is eye-clean in nearly every radiant cut — the brilliant facet pattern is effective at concealing inclusions across its many small facets.

SI1 can work if the GIA clarity plot shows the inclusion positioned near the girdle edge (not under the table) and if you confirm with a face-up video. Never buy SI2 or below without a face-up video in natural daylight — the certificate plot does not tell you whether the inclusion catches the eye.

For lab-grown, VVS1 and VS1 are common and affordable — the same clarity logic applies, but the price gap between VS2 and VVS1 in lab-grown is small enough that stepping up is worth it.

### Proportions: The Numbers That Matter Most

This is where most buyers skip a step and where the most preventable mistakes happen. GIA does not assign an overall cut grade to radiant cut diamonds — you must read the proportion data from the "Measurements" and "Proportions" sections of the certificate directly.

**Table %:** Target 61–69%. Below 56% reduces brilliance. Above 73% concentrates light in the center and kills fire.

**Depth %:** Target 61–67%. This is the most important single number. Above 70%, carat weight is hidden below the girdle — the stone measures heavier than it looks face-up. A 1ct at 73% depth looks noticeably smaller than a 1ct at 63% depth.

**L:W ratio:** Divide the length measurement by the width measurement from the certificate. 1.25–1.35 is the most popular elongated range. 1.00–1.05 is square. 1.35+ is very elongated.

For the full proportion chart, face-up size by carat, and depth/table guide, see [radiant cut diamond ideal proportions](/radiant-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

![Loose radiant cut diamond proportion checklist — table %, depth %, L:W ratio, certification, and face-up video guide](/images/loose-radiant-cut-diamond-proportion-checklist.avif)

### Polish and Symmetry

Target Very Good or Excellent for both. Good polish or symmetry signals inconsistency in the cutting process — it shows in the stone's light return and can cause slight visual irregularities face-up. Neither replaces the proportion check: a stone can have Excellent polish and Excellent symmetry at 73% depth, which is still a poorly proportioned stone.

## Loose Radiant Cut vs. Other Loose Diamond Shapes

When you buy loose, you are also choosing a shape. Radiant cut competes directly with oval, cushion, princess, and emerald as elongated or fancy loose stone options. The differences matter — in proportions, certification, face-up behavior, and price.

| Shape | Ideal Table % | Ideal Depth % | GIA Cut Grade? | 1ct Natural Entry | Face-Up at 1ct (approx.) | What this means |
|-------|--------------|---------------|----------------|-------------------|--------------------------|---------|
| Radiant Cut | 61–69% | 61–67% | No | $2,318 G-VS2 | 6.5×4.9mm (1.25:1 ratio) | Brilliant sparkle in rectangular form. No GIA cut grade — must read proportions. |
| Oval Cut | 53–63% | 58–62% | No | ~$2,100 G-VS2 | 8.2×5.5mm (1.50:1 ratio) | Most face-up area of elongated shapes. Bow-tie risk at certain depths. |
| Cushion Cut | 56–65% | 61–68% | No | ~$2,000 G-VS2 | ~6.2×5.8mm | Soft corners, brilliant or modified brilliant facets. Less elongation than radiant. |
| Princess Cut | 62–69% | 64–75% | No | ~$2,200 G-VS2 | ~5.5×5.5mm | Square. Higher acceptable depth than radiant — pavilion is steeper by design. |
| Emerald Cut | 60–70% | 61–67% | No | ~$2,400 G-VS2 | ~6.9×4.8mm | Step-cut facets — inclusions are more visible; requires VS2 minimum. No crushed-ice effect. |
| Round Brilliant | 54–58% | 59–63% | Yes — Excellent | ~$3,230 G-VS2 | ~6.4mm diameter | Maximum face-up per carat. Only shape with a reliable GIA cut grade shortcut. Highest natural price. |

**What this means:** Radiant cut is one of the most affordable natural loose stone options at 1ct — cheaper than round brilliant ($3,230) and emerald ($2,400) for the same grade tier, and comparable to oval and cushion. The absence of a GIA cut grade is the same for all fancy shapes — radiant, oval, cushion, princess, and emerald all require certificate proportion review. Round brilliant is the only shape where GIA "Excellent" simplifies the buying decision.

**Verdict:** If you want maximum face-up size per carat in an elongated shape, oval shows more face-up area than radiant at the same carat weight (shallower pavilion, longer outline). If you want brilliant sparkle in a rectangular shape with corner protection, radiant is the best option. If you want step-cut clarity and hall-of-mirrors effect, emerald is the choice — but inclusions are more visible and require a higher clarity grade. See [radiant cut vs emerald cut diamond](/radiant-cut-vs-emerald-cut-diamond) for the direct comparison.

![Loose diamond shape comparison face-up at 1 carat — radiant, oval, cushion, princess, emerald, and round brilliant measurements and entry prices](/images/loose-radiant-cut-diamond-shape-comparison.avif)

