> **Farzana says:**
>
> People see an EGL-certified stone for $900 less and think they found a deal. They did not. That $900 is what the market discounts EGL for. You will pay more to reset that stone with a reputable jeweler, and get less if you ever try to sell it. GIA for natural. IGI for lab. That is the whole rule.

## Radiant vs Lab Radiant — Grade-for-Grade Comparison

This table shows natural vs lab at comparable grades side by side, so the price difference is clear in real numbers.

| Carat | Natural Grade | Natural Price | Lab Grade | Lab Price | Price Diff | What this means |
|-------|--------------|---------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------------|
| 1ct | G-VS2 GIA | [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VVS1 IGI | [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | −$1,451 | Lab is better grade at 63% less. |
| 1ct | D-VVS2 GIA | [$3,594](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29587972?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | D-VVS1 IGI | [$932](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24946874?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | −$2,662 | Lab is 74% cheaper at nearly equivalent grade. |
| 1.5ct | G-VS2 GIA | [$7,704](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29651855?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VVS1 IGI | [$1,600](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24979338?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | −$6,104 | Lab is better grade at 79% less. |
| 2ct | D-VS2 GIA | [$17,714](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/16750163?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VVS1 IGI | [$2,220](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | −$15,494 | Lab is 87% cheaper. The 2ct price gap is the most striking in the market. |

**What this means:** The price gap is not explained by quality — lab-grown radiant cut diamonds have the same hardness, refractive index, and crystal structure as natural. The gap reflects rarity and formation process. Natural diamonds are mined from the earth; lab diamonds are grown in weeks. The market prices that difference heavily.

**Verdict:** If you are deciding between natural and lab, the question is not quality. It is whether the natural origin — the rarity, the geological history, the heirloom value, and the resale trust — is worth the premium to you. Neither answer is wrong. But the premium is real, measurable, and compounds with carat weight.

## The Value Zones: Where to Buy in the Market

The loose radiant cut diamond market has three clear value zones regardless of origin.

**Zone 1 — Best value per appearance:** G-VS2 to F-VS1 in natural ([$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)–[$2,787](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29538475?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at 1ct), D-VVS1 to E-VVS1 in lab ([$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)–[$932](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24946874?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at 1ct). These grades hit the eye-clean threshold and near-colorless range without paying for certificate upgrades that do not show face-up.

**Zone 2 — Certificate premium:** D-VVS2 and above in natural, D-FL in lab. The stone looks identical to Zone 1 face-up, but the certificate reads better. Relevant for buyers who value resale, insurance documentation, or heirloom quality. Not the best choice if visual performance is the only goal.

**Zone 3 — Rarity premium:** 2ct+ natural. The per-carat cost increases 3–4× per carat above 1ct. The face-up size increase does not match the price increase. A 2ct natural costs nearly 4× more per carat than a 1ct, but is only about 27% wider face-up.

![Loose radiant cut diamond 3 value zones — Zone 1 best value, Zone 2 certificate premium, Zone 3 rarity premium with natural and lab price ranges](/images/loose-radiant-cut-diamond-value-zones.avif)

## Proportion Checklist for Any Loose Radiant Cut

Before confirming any loose radiant cut purchase, verify these from the certificate.

- → **Table %:** 61–69%. This is the first number to read. Above 73% is a hard pass.
- → **Depth %:** 61–67%. Above 70% means the stone appears smaller than its weight. Request a video for any stone above 68%.
- → **L:W ratio:** Divide length by width from the certificate measurements. Confirm it matches your shape preference.
- → **Girdle:** Thin to Medium or Medium to Slightly Thick. Avoid Very Thin or Extremely Thick.
- → **Culet:** None or Very Small. A medium culet shows as a dark circle face-up.
- → **Polish and Symmetry:** Very Good or Excellent for both.
- → **Certification:** GIA for natural, IGI for lab. Nothing else.
- → **Face-up video:** For any stone above $1,500, request a daylight video before confirming.

> **Farzana says:**
>
> The proportion checklist takes five minutes. Most buyers skip it. Three months later, they message me asking why their stone looks smaller than expected. Depth % is the answer, almost every time. Read it before the grade.

## 4 Buyer Scenarios

**The 1ct natural engagement ring buyer:** Budget is $2,300–$2,800. You want GIA certification and a stone you can insure and resize. The 1ct G-VS2 at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) or F-VS1 at [$2,787](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29538475?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the right zone — eye-clean, near-colorless, GIA-certified. Check table and depth on the certificate before picking. For setting options, see [radiant cut diamond settings](/radiant-cut-diamond-settings).

**The size-first lab buyer:** You want a 2ct face-up presence at the smallest possible cost. A lab 2ct E-VVS1 IGI at [$2,220](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers 2ct face-up area (approximately 8.5×6.4mm at 63% depth) at a top-tier grade — for the price of a 1ct natural entry. Confirm table and depth before selecting; lab-grown proportions vary just as much as natural.

**The elongated shape buyer:** You want the most finger-elongating look possible in a loose radiant. Look specifically for a 1.30–1.40 L:W ratio in the measurements. A 1ct at 1.35:1 measures approximately 7.0×4.8mm face-up versus 6.5×4.9mm at 1.25:1. The 0.10 ratio step makes a visible shape difference on the finger. See the [elongated radiant cut diamond](/elongated-radiant-cut-diamond) guide for the full ratio comparison.

**The upgrade buyer — 1ct to 1.5ct natural:** You already have a 1ct natural radiant and are ready to upgrade. The 1.5ct E-VS1 GIA at [$7,844](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28090460?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — only $140 more than the G-VS2 entry — gives you a meaningful face-up size increase (approximately 7.5×5.7mm vs 6.5×4.9mm at 1ct) with a stronger grade. For the [1 carat radiant cut diamond ring](/1-carat-radiant-cut-diamond-ring) and [2 carat radiant cut diamond ring](/2-carat-radiant-cut-diamond-ring) guides, see the linked posts for ring-specific sizing and setting advice.

