**What this means:** Natural and lab serve different buyer profiles. Natural buyers are paying for rarity, origin, and resale stability. Lab buyers are paying for maximum grade and size per dollar. The proportion checklist applies equally to both — a poorly proportioned stone is a poorly proportioned stone regardless of origin or certification.

**Verdict:** For most buyers, the decision lands in Zone 1 of whichever origin fits: natural G-VS2 GIA at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), or lab D-VVS1 IGI at [$932](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24946874?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Both are eye-clean, well-certified, and will perform well in any radiant cut setting. Check depth % before you confirm either one.

## Expert Summary

Loose radiant cut diamonds give you the most control over what you are buying. You read the certificate before committing — table %, depth %, L:W ratio — and pick the stone before the setting. That order matters.

Natural GIA stones start at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a 1ct G-VS2. Lab IGI stones start at [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a 1ct E-VVS1. The 2ct lab at [$2,220](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26351307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs less than a natural 1ct entry — that gap is real and compounds at every carat step.

The single number to check before any purchase: depth %. Keep it at 61–67%. Specific stone IDs in this guide may sell — apply the same grade and proportion criteria to the next available stone. The numbers that do not expire: table 61–69%, depth 61–67%, GIA for natural, IGI for lab.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a loose diamond and why buy one?

A loose diamond is sold without a setting — just the stone, with a certificate. Buying loose gives you full control over the stone selection before choosing where to set it.

You can compare proportions, read the certificate, shortlist multiple stones at different prices, and make the setting decision separately. Most buyers who care about quality buy loose.

### What certification should I require for a loose radiant cut diamond?

GIA for natural radiant cut diamonds. IGI for lab-grown. EGL is not accepted — its grading is documented to run 1–2 color grades and 1–2 clarity grades above GIA equivalent.

An EGL-certified stone is priced by the market as if it were 1–2 grades lower than stated. Do not buy uncertified natural stones unless you have independent GIA appraisal.

### Is a loose lab-grown radiant the same quality as natural?

Chemically and physically, yes. Both are carbon in a crystal structure — same hardness (10 Mohs), same refractive index, same fire and brilliance at equivalent proportions. The difference is origin and how they formed.

Lab-grown diamonds are not simulants (moissanite, CZ) — they are real diamonds grown in a controlled environment.

### Why does GIA not issue a cut grade for radiant cut diamonds?

GIA only issues an overall cut grade for round brilliant diamonds, where an ideal proportion model exists and is mathematically defined. Fancy shapes — radiant, oval, cushion, princess, emerald — vary in intended style (square vs elongated, crushed-ice vs step-cut) in ways that cannot be captured by a single ideal proportion model. For fancy shapes, you read the proportion numbers from the certificate yourself.

### What depth percentage should I target for a loose radiant cut diamond?

61–67%. This is the range where carat weight is visible in the face-up area rather than hidden below the girdle. A stone at 73% depth will look smaller than its carat weight suggests.

A stone at 63% depth shows maximum face-up size per carat. Depth % is the single most important number to check on a radiant cut certificate.

### How is a loose radiant cut different from a loose oval or cushion?

The shape outline and facet arrangement differ. Radiant cut has cropped corners and a hybrid brilliant facet pattern — it elongates but with a rectangular face-up. Oval has pointed ends with no corners and typically shows more face-up area per carat.

Cushion has rounded corners and a softer look. All three require manual proportion review — none have GIA cut grades. See [cushion cut vs radiant cut diamond](/cushion-cut-vs-radiant-cut-diamond) for the direct comparison.

### What L:W ratio is most popular for loose radiant cut diamonds?

1.25–1.35 is the most requested range, and 1.30:1 is the single most popular individual ratio. This creates the classic elongated rectangular look without going into very-stretched territory. A 1ct at 1.30:1 measures roughly 6.7×5.0mm face-up at 63% depth.

### What is the minimum clarity I should buy for a loose radiant cut?

VS2 for natural, VS1 or VVS2 for lab (since lab-grown VVS grades cost nearly the same as VS grades). In a radiant cut, VS2 is eye-clean for most stones because the crushed-ice pattern scatters inclusions across many small facets. SI1 can work if you review the clarity plot and confirm the inclusion is at the girdle edge — not the table center — and you verify with a face-up video.

### Can I buy a loose radiant cut and set it somewhere besides where I purchased it?

Yes. A GIA or IGI certificate belongs to the stone, not the retailer. You can take any certified loose stone to any jeweler who offers setting services.

Many buyers purchase loose from one source and have it set by a local jeweler they trust, or through an online jeweler who offers custom setting work.

### Is a square radiant cut the same stone as a rectangular one?

Both are radiant cut diamonds — the only difference is the L:W ratio. A square radiant has an L:W of 1.00–1.05. A rectangular radiant has an L:W above 1.10.

The same certification requirements, proportion targets, and grading rules apply to both. See the [square radiant cut diamond](/square-radiant-cut-diamond) guide for ratio-specific buying advice.

### How much does a 1ct loose natural radiant cut diamond cost?

From [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a GIA-certified G-VS2 to [$3,594](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29587972?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for D-VVS2, based on certified market inventory {{month_year}}. The G-VS2 to F-VS1 range ([$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)–[$2,787](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29538475?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)) is where most 1ct buyers land for the best value-to-appearance ratio.

### What should I ask a retailer before buying a loose radiant cut?

Ask for the full GIA or IGI certificate (not just a report summary). Confirm the table %, depth %, and L:W ratio from the measurements section. Ask for a face-up video in daylight for any stone above $1,500.

Ask whether the stone has been inspected for clarity plot accuracy — inclusions should match the certificate diagram. If a retailer cannot provide a face-up video, buy from one who can.

